<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Pella</h1><p class="st-meta">1800 BCE – 1150 BCE (approx.) · capital Pella</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Pella, a Canaanite town in the Jordan Valley known to the Egyptians as Pihilu, whose rulers wrote to the pharaoh in the Amarna age.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Pihilu stood at Tabaqat Fahl, beside a perennial spring above the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hashemite-kingdom-of-jordan">Jordan</a> Valley, inhabited since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-bronze-age">Middle Bronze Age</a> it became a walled town, named in Egyptian execration texts, and controlled farmland and the route from the valley into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/emirate-of-transjordan">Transjordan</a> highlands.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-sydney">University of Sydney</a> excavations since 1979 have uncovered fortifications, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> governor's residence and a temple. In the 14th century BCE its ruler Mut-Bahlu wrote to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a>, and Akkadian tablets have been found at the site.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Canaanite town declined with Egyptian power in the 12th century BCE. Pella revived in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a> and became one of the cities of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/decapolis-levant">Decapolis (Levant)</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/pella.jpg" alt="Pella" width="320" height="151"><figcaption>Image: Jean-Pol GRANDMONT · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:0_Mosa%C3%AFque_de_la_chasse_au_lion_-_Mus%C3%A9e_arch%C3%A9ologique_de_Pella.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1800 BCE – 1150 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century-bce">19th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century-bce">12th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Pella</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mut-Bahlu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Canaanite religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Canaanite, Akkadian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/jordan">Jordan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Pihilu, Tabaqat Fahl</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-1475&amp;focus=pella" aria-label="Pella on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5595 998 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Pella lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5915 1254L5863 1489"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5863 1489h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5915 1254h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5915 1254h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Pella. <a href="/atlas?year=-1475&amp;focus=pella">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

